I had no time to dig deep into the next.js css-modules implementation, but wanted to share this: 

- [postcss-modules-values](https://github.com/css-modules/css-modules/blob/master/docs/values-variables.md) is messed up and doesn't even work in next.js
- [next.js doesn't let you configure this plugin](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/blob/master/errors/postcss-ignored-plugin.md)
- My use case was to declare module variables. As a workaround I used [custom CSS properties (variables)](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Using_CSS_custom_properties) in the top level module class like so:
```
.container {
    --color-fancy: #00deee;
    --color-primary: #3b9aa1;
}
```
Then simply apply them in your descendants module classes:
`.foo { color: var(--color-fancy); }`

If you want to use separate file for the variables, put the top level class that contains variables in a separate file and then compose:

```
.foo {
  composes: container from "./colors.css";
  color: var(--color-fancy);
}
```

Overriding the default webpack config worked for me.

To override import behaviour for `.module.css` (or sass -- thanks to @nkalinov via [this comment](https://github.com/vercel/next.js/issues/11629#issuecomment-623511124])):

#### next.config.js
```js
/**
 * Stolen from https://stackoverflow.com/questions/10776600/testing-for-equality-of-regular-expressions
 */
const regexEqual = (x, y) => {
  return (
    x instanceof RegExp &&
    y instanceof RegExp &&
    x.source === y.source &&
    x.global === y.global &&
    x.ignoreCase === y.ignoreCase &&
    x.multiline === y.multiline
  );
};

module.exports = {
  webpack: (config) => {
    const oneOf = config.module.rules.find(
      (rule) => typeof rule.oneOf === 'object'
    );

