I wonder if the more elegant solution here is to expose the ability to choose the trailing slash behavior on a route-by-route basis. The suggested behavior of setting `undefined` and having Next simply respond to both `example.com/test/` and `example.com/test` with the _same content_ may actually be quite hurtful to the site's SEO values ([ref - 'Avoid duplicate content'](https://en.ryte.com/wiki/Trailing_Slashes#Trailing_slashes_and_SEO), [ref - 'Avoid...pages contenting with each other'](https://www.safaridigital.com.au/blog/trailing-slash-seo/), [ref - Google's John Mueller](https://twitter.com/JohnMu/status/943076424130363392)) so generally I think this is something Next would probably want to avoid exposing users open to.

A route-by-route approach (with globbing / splatting) that implements an implicit inheritance-by-order may provide a more versatile approach that could solve a lot of folks' issues described in this thread (IMO). Might look something like:

E.g.

```js
