This is unrelated to next. You have a several syntax errors...

## Issue 1
You have  invalid CSS syntax: `soan: { ... }` or `span: { ... }` is not valid. Instead you can select `<span>` with just `span { ... }` (selector `#span` refers to an `id`, which doesn't exist as an attribute to your `<span>` and while selector `& > span` is valid, the reason it didn't work is related to issue 2 mentioned below).

An even better approach would be to assign a `className` to the `<span>` element and target the `className`. 

For example:
 ```jsx
<span className="example">dummy</span>
```
and then selecting the class name using:
```css
& .example { 
   font-family: "Noto Sans Georgian Bold", sans-serif;
}
```
This is better for **flexibility/reusability** and **specificity**.

Anyway, here's the corrected syntax:
```jsx
import styled from "styled-components";

const SectionTiTle = styled.h1`
  color: #343d47;

  span { 
    font-family: "Noto Sans Georgian Bold", sans-serif;
  }
`;

export default SectionTtile;
```
## Issue 2 
Your `font-family` names have to match.

You'll need to globally import the font and specify the `font-family` naming **and** specify the correct `url` -- this can either be a CDN url: `https://example.com` or if it's in your project's `public` folder, then using Next's [static-file serving](https://nextjs.org/docs/basic-features/static-file-serving), you can import with `/`:

```css
@font-face {
  font-family: "Noto Sans Georgian Bold";
  font-style: normal;
  font-weight: bold;
  src: url("/NotoSansGeorgian-Bold.ttf") format("truetype");
}
```

